// ExplainAccessWithResponse What a person holds at a place, and why
//
// The roles that count for the person at the workspace, a hub or a collection, where each is
// held, and every permission they carry. Given `permission`, also whether the person holds
// it and through which role - the answer behind "view as" and "why may this person do
// that". A person may always ask about themselves; about somebody else it takes
// `MANAGE_MEMBERS` or `READ_CONFIGURATION` at the workspace and, for a hub or a collection,
// `READ` at that place - a place the caller does not reach answers not found.
//
// Returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with GET /access/explain (the `ExplainAccess` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) ExplainAccessWithResponse(ctx context.Context, params *ExplainAccessParams, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*ExplainAccessResult, error) {
	rsp, err := c.ExplainAccess(ctx, params, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseExplainAccessResult(rsp)
}

// GetOwnAccountWithResponse The account the caller is signed in as
//
// Who am I, and how does this product speak to me. A client needs the answer before it can
// render anything: `locale`, `time_zone` and `week_start` are the second link of the
// resolution chain request → account → tenant → installation, and there
// is no other way to learn them — `/accounts/{accountId}/preferences` writes to an id the
// client never receives.
//
// A service account gets the same document rather than a refusal. It has an `Account` row
// with `kind: SERVICE_ACCOUNT`, and answering "you are a machine, and here is which one" is
// more useful to a caller than a 403 it has to special-case.
//
// `me` is a **reserved segment**: no account identifier is ever the string `me`, because
// identifiers are UUIDs. The two paths cannot collide, and this is written down because a
// reader looking at `/accounts/{accountId}` beside it will otherwise wonder.
