// ReadOauthClientWithResponse What an app is called, for the person being asked to allow it
//
// The half of a registered client a consent screen needs: its name. Any signed-in member of the workspace may read it, and that is the point - the person deciding whether to allow an app is usually not an administrator, and a consent screen that cannot name the app is a screen that asks somebody to approve an identifier. That is the shape a phishing attempt wants.
// Deliberately narrower than the listing. The registered redirect URIs and whether the client is confidential are the administrator's business and are answered by `GET /oauth/clients` behind the permission that manages members; nothing here helps somebody enumerate the workspace's apps, because reading one requires already knowing its identifier - which the authorization request the reader arrived with already carries.
