// SearchItemsWithBodyWithResponse Full-text search over the entries a caller may see
//
// Finds entries by the words in their titles and notes, best match first. A `POST` for the
// reason the query is one - the request is a document rather than a set of parameters - and
// it reads: nothing is written, and the same request may be repeated.
//
// Two languages are in play and they are different questions. An entry is indexed under the
// language it was written in (`content_language`), so a German entry is found by an inflected
// German word and an English one by a stemmed English word. The *query* is read under the
// caller's language, which is this request's `language` or the caller's locale. An entry that
// states no language is matched word by word and is found by any caller. Which languages this
// installation can index at all is answered by `GET /meta/capabilities` under
// `text_languages`; a language it has no configuration for searches word by word rather than
// failing.
//
// A script without word boundaries - Japanese, Chinese, Korean, Thai - additionally matches on
// substrings, because a run of such characters is one token and no word search can find part
// of it.
//
// Unanchored unless a container is named, and this is the one read of the API that is: it
// answers what the caller may see rather than refusing what they may not, so a page can be
// shorter than the size asked for. Walk on until `has_more` is false rather than stopping at
// the first short page. Naming a hub is the exception - a client that named a hub it cannot
// read is told so.
//
// A `POST` and no `GET`. What somebody is looking for is their content, and a query string
// travels through access logs, proxies and browser history; the same words in a body do
// not. What is searched is entries - tasks, work packages and activities - which is what
// `searchItems` says.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with POST /search (the `SearchItems` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) SearchItemsWithBodyWithResponse(ctx context.Context, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*SearchItemsResult, error) {
	rsp, err := c.SearchItemsWithBody(ctx, contentType, body, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseSearchItemsResult(rsp)
}

